def get_slide_data() -> dict:
    return {
        'title': 'Execution 1 — Timing, Steps & Sample Progress',
        'content': '\n## Execution 1: What Does One Full Evaluation Look Like?\n\nThe StellarOrion pipeline runs the following sequence **once per CCD sample** (plus once for the baseline):\n\n```\nGeometry Engine      SPARTA DSMC (10 cores)      PINN Refinement      OpenFOAM laplacianFoam\n─────────────────  →  ─────────────────────────  →  ─────────────────  →  ──────────────────────\nGenerate .surf file   1100 steps × mpirun -np 10   1500 iterations       Solid heat conduction\n     ~3 s                    ~42 min                   ~11 min                ~8–47 min\n```\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1 — Baseline Validation (One-Time)\n\nThis is the **IRVE-3 reference geometry** ($D = 3.0$ m, $\\theta = 60°$, $N = 6$, $R_n = 0.55$ m) simulated first to anchor all subsequent comparisons.\n\n| Sub-phase | Wall time (observed) | Notes |\n|:---|:---:|:---|\n| SPARTA DSMC baseline | **~42 min** | 1100 steps, 10 cores, ~1.07M particles |\n| Animation encoding (6 MP4s) | ~7 min | Temp, velocity, Mach, pressure, Knudsen, grid |\n| OpenFOAM laplacianFoam (1st) | ~9 min | `blockMesh` + `snappyHexMesh` + parallel solve |\n| OpenFOAM laplacianFoam (2nd) | ~1 min | Reconstructed case post-process |\n| Post-processing + PINN (Phase 1.2) | **~11 min** | DeepXDE MPS training, 1500 iterations |\n| **Total Phase 1** | **~70 min** | Single baseline only — done once |\n\n---\n\n## What Are the 1100 SPARTA Steps?\n\nEach SPARTA "step" is one **DSMC time increment** $\\Delta t$, chosen so that the mean particle free-flight distance matches the cell size ($\\Delta x \\approx \\lambda_{mfp}$).\n\n$$\\Delta t_{DSMC} \\approx \\frac{\\lambda_{mfp}}{v_{thermal}} \\approx \\frac{10^{-3} \\text{ m}}{1650 \\text{ m/s}} \\approx 6 \\times 10^{-7} \\text{ s} \\approx 0.6 \\; \\mu\\text{s}$$\n\nSo **1100 steps ≈ 0.66 ms of simulated physical time** — not 11 ms. This is typical for DSMC: the code reaches a **statistical steady state** within ~400–500 steps, then runs the remaining ~600 steps to average the surface fluxes for convergence. The `Loop time of 2518 s` is real wall-clock time on 10 CPU cores.\n\n**Convergence check:** $C_D$ and $\\dot{q}_{stag}$ are averaged over all 11 dump files (steps 100–1100). The coefficient of variation (CV) of drag across dumps gives the uncertainty: CV = 7.3% in the current run — acceptable for surrogate training.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2 — CCD Samples (25 Evaluations)\n\nEach of the 25 CCD samples runs a **faster evaluation** (no PINN, less animation encoding):\n\n| Sub-phase | Estimated time | Notes |\n|:---|:---:|:---|\n| Geometry engine + mesh | ~3 s | HIAD_GeometryEngine.py → .surf file |\n| SPARTA DSMC (sample) | ~35–45 min | Same 1100 steps, geometry varies |\n| OpenFOAM thermal | ~5–15 min | Solid conduction, 60–450 s sim time |\n| **Total per sample** | **~45–65 min** | |\n| **Total for all 25** | **~20–27 hours** | Running overnight on idle cycles |\n\n**Current progress (from `StellarOrionIntentionLog.jsonl`):**\n\n> 🟡 **Active sample:** $D = 3.36$ m, $\\theta = 60°$, $N = 6$, $R_n = 0.55$ m (SPARTA Step 800/1100 as of last log entry)\n\nThe optimization loop started at **07:35** and Sample 1 began immediately. Based on the SPARTA timing (≈ 42 min/sample), we expect:\n- **Samples completed by morning:** ~4–6 of 25 depending on OpenFOAM duration\n- **Full 25-sample run:** needs ~1–1.5 more overnight cycles\n\n---\n\n## OpenFOAM Simulation Time vs Physical Validity\n\nThe OpenFOAM `laplacianFoam` solves **transient solid heat conduction** over `endTime` seconds of simulated physical time:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial T}{\\partial t} = D_T \\nabla^2 T, \\quad D_T = \\frac{k}{\\rho c_p} = 6.57 \\times 10^{-8} \\text{ m}^2\\text{/s}$$\n\nWith $D_T \\approx 6.57 \\times 10^{-8}$ m²/s and TPS thickness $L \\approx 3.6$ mm:\n\n$$t_{soak} \\approx \\frac{L^2}{D_T} = \\frac{(3.6 \\times 10^{-3})^2}{6.57 \\times 10^{-8}} \\approx \\mathbf{197 \\text{ s}} \\approx 3.3 \\text{ min of entry time}$$\n\n**This means:** `endTime = 450 s` in OpenFOAM represents ~450 seconds of real entry flight time — the thermal soak from peak heating through the HIAD descent. The observed **wall-clock time** of 8–47 min is just the solver running fast/slow depending on mesh resolution and parallel load.\n\n---\n\n## Are Different OpenFOAM Times Valid for Comparison?\n\n**Yes, but with caveats.** Since $T(x, t)$ is a time-dependent field, samples with different `endTime` values are measuring the shield at different moments during entry. When we compare CCD samples:\n\n| Condition | Interpretation | Action |\n|:---|:---|:---|\n| Same `endTime` across all samples | Direct comparison valid ✅ | Use as-is |\n| Different `endTime` (e.g. one sample crashed/resumed) | Comparison needs normalisation ⚠️ | Extract $T_{backface}$ at the same physical time |\n| `endTime` matches $t_{soak}$ | Most physically meaningful ✅ | Confirmed thermal equilibrium |\n\nStellarOrion currently uses `endTime = env_duration = 450 s` uniformly for all samples (set in `controlDict`). So all 25 CCD samples are comparable at the same physical moment of entry.\n\n**Conclusion so far:** The baseline validation shows $T_{surface} = 1453$ K (within the 1870 K SiC limit), $T_{backface} = 492$ K (well below 773 K Kapton limit). The framework is physically valid. The CCD sweep will tell us how these temperatures vary across the design space.\n'
    }
